1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,577
Section eleven: snapshots.

2
00:00:02,857 --> 00:00:04,862
Trips never change once they happen.

3
00:00:05,142 --> 00:00:09,985
But some data is updated in place, and when that happens, the old value is gone.

4
00:00:10,265 --> 00:00:11,502
Snapshots keep it.

5
00:00:12,602 --> 00:00:15,184
Think of an address, a price, or a vendor name.

6
00:00:15,463 --> 00:00:23,656
The source system overwrites it, and yesterday's value is lost, unless we keep one row per version, with the dates each version was valid.

7
00:00:23,936 --> 00:00:26,581
That is a slowly changing dimension, type two.

8
00:00:27,681 --> 00:00:29,387
This really happened in our data.

9
00:00:29,668 --> 00:00:34,190
Older versions of the taxi data dictionary say vendor two is VeriFone.

10
00:00:34,470 --> 00:00:36,369
The current one says Curb Mobility.

11
00:00:36,649 --> 00:00:39,273
And two new vendors appeared, Myle and Helix.

12
00:00:40,373 --> 00:00:42,378
The snapshots page, version one.

13
00:00:42,658 --> 00:00:48,504
Since dbt one point nine, a snapshot is a YAML file pointing at a source or model.

14
00:00:48,784 --> 00:00:58,768
It needs a unique key and a strategy. dbt adds valid from and valid to columns, and snapshots ignore full refresh, so history is never wiped.

15
00:00:59,867 --> 00:01:00,998
Two strategies.

16
00:01:01,278 --> 00:01:05,780
Timestamp compares an updated at column, when the source has a reliable one.

17
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:09,174
Check compares the actual values of the columns you list.

18
00:01:09,454 --> 00:01:12,868
Our vendor list has no timestamp, so we check the vendor name.

19
00:01:13,967 --> 00:01:16,399
First we need a source system that changes.

20
00:01:16,679 --> 00:01:24,573
In the demo, the agent creates a small vendor registry table with the old list: Creative Mobile Technologies, and VeriFone.

21
00:01:25,673 --> 00:01:33,609
Then the agent adds the table to our source, creates the snapshot in YAML with the check strategy, and runs dbt snapshot.

22
00:01:33,889 --> 00:01:36,641
Many examples online use an older syntax.

23
00:01:36,921 --> 00:01:38,222
Our skill says YAML.

24
00:01:39,322 --> 00:01:45,231
Seven lines: a name, the source it reads, a unique key, the check strategy, and the column to check.

25
00:01:45,511 --> 00:01:50,141
If the agent writes the old syntax, we discard it, and add the rule to the skill.

26
00:01:51,241 --> 00:01:56,318
After the first snapshot, both vendors are current, and their valid to column is empty.

27
00:01:57,418 --> 00:01:59,615
Then someone updates the vendor system.

28
00:01:59,895 --> 00:02:06,679
Vendor two becomes Curb Mobility, and vendors six and seven are added, exactly as in the real dictionary.

29
00:02:06,959 --> 00:02:09,029
We run dbt snapshot again.

30
00:02:10,129 --> 00:02:11,217
History, kept.

31
00:02:11,497 --> 00:02:18,259
Vendor two now has two rows: VeriFone, valid until the second run, and Curb Mobility, valid from then on.

32
00:02:18,539 --> 00:02:22,443
Any report can now ask what a vendor was called at the time of a trip.

33
00:02:23,543 --> 00:02:29,688
On the graph: the new source, the snapshot, and a vendor dimension built from the current rows of the snapshot.
